4967   
4968  *------------------------------------------------------------------
4969  *2.5.10  EV and CV welfare measures
4970  *------------------------------------------------------------------
4971   
4972  PARAMETER
4973   IC(H,SIM)              indirect compensation function by household H and simulation SIM
4974   EV(AC,SIM)             equivalent variation by household AC and simulation SIM
4975   BETAAUX(H)             auxiliary term based on marginal spending shares
4976   PRICEIND(H,SIM)        price index
4977   EXPSUB(H,SIM)          subsistence spending for household H under simulation SIM
4978   INDUTIL(H,SIM)         indirect utility for household H under simulation SIM
4979   EXPV0(H,SIM)           base-utility expenditure function for household H under simulation SIM
4980   CV(AC,SIM)             compensating variation for household H under simulation SIM
4981   CV2(AC,SIM)            concise formula for CV
4982   EVX(AC,SIM)            disaggregated EV results
4983  ;
4984   
       Source for IC and EV formulas (here adapted to fit demand system with home and market consumption):
       Blonigen, Bruce A., Joseph E. Flynn, and Kenneth A. Reinert (1997) Sector-Focused General Equilibrium
       Modeling, pp. 189-230 in eds. Joseph F. Francois and Kenneth A. Reinert, Applied Methods for Trade
       Policy Analysis. Cambridge, MA: Cambridge University Press. Formulas are on pp. 224-225.
       
       Sadoulet, E., and A. de Janvry (1995). Quantitative Development Policy Analysis. The Johns Hopkins
       University Press: Baltimore and London. Section 1.5.1.3, pp. 13-14.
       
       Note: CV is measured at simulated prices and incomes. It shows: the maximum payment the consumer
       would be willing to make to avoid having the simulated change undone (i.e. the payment after which
       the consumer would have been just as well off as without the change).For positive (negative) welfare
       change, CV > 0 (< 0).
       
       Note: EV is measured at base prices and incomes. It shows: the minimum payment that the consumer
       would require for foregoing a change (i.e., if the consumer receives this payment, then he/she is as
       well off under base prices and income as he/she would have been if the change had taken place.) For
       positive (negative) welfare change, CV > 0 (< 0).
       
5005   
5006  *Indirect compensation
5007  *   IC measure the consumption value needed at base prices to generate same welfare as under prices and income under SIM
5008   IC(H,SIMC)
5009      = ((x_EH(H,SIMC) - SUM(C, x_PQ(C,SIMC)*gammam(C,H)) - SUM(A, x_PA(A,SIMC)*gammah(A,H)))  / (PROD(C, x_PQ(C,SIMC)**betam(C,H)) * PROD(A, x_PA(A,SIMC)**betah(A,H))))
5010      * (PROD(C, PQ0(C)**betam(C,H)) * PROD(A, PA0(A)**betah(A,H))) +  SUM(C, PQ0(C)*gammam(C,H)) + SUM(A, PA0(A)*gammah(A,H));
5011   
5012  *Equivalent variation
5013  *  EV measures the income change that, at base prices, would be equivalent to the change simulated for SIM
5014   EV(H,SIMC) = IC(H,SIMC) - EH0(H);
5015   EV('TOTAL',SIMC) = SUM(H, EV(H,SIMC));
5016  *  Disaggregated EV results
5017   EVX(H,'BASE')       = EH0(H);
5018   EVX('TOTAL','BASE') = SUM(H, EH0(H));
5019   EVX(H,SIMNB)$EH0(H) = EV(H,SIMNB) / EH0(H) * 100;
5020   EVX('TOTAL',SIMNB)$SUM(H, EH0(H)) = SUM(H, EV(H,SIMNB)) / SUM(H, EH0(H)) * 100;
5021   
5022  *Compensating variation
5023  *   "ALPHA"
5024   BETAAUX(H)       = PROD(C, betam(C,H)**betam(C,H))   * PROD(A, betah(A,H)**betah(A,H));
5025   PRICEIND(H,SIMC)  = PROD(C, x_PQ(C,SIMC)**betam(C,H)) * PROD(A, x_PA(A,SIMC)**betah(A,H));
5026   EXPSUB(H,SIMC)    = SUM(C, x_PQ(C,SIMC)*gammam(C,H))  + SUM(A, x_PA(A,SIMC)*gammah(A,H));
5027  *   "V"
5028   INDUTIL(H,SIMC)   = BETAAUX(H) *(x_EH(H,SIMC) - EXPSUB(H,SIMC)) / PRICEIND(H,SIMC);
5029  *   "e(p1,u0)"
5030   EXPV0(H,SIMC)     = (INDUTIL(H,'BASE')/BETAAUX(H)) * PRICEIND(H,SIMC) + EXPSUB(H,SIMC);
5031   CV(H,SIMC)        = x_EH(H,SIMC) - EXPV0(H,SIMC);
5032   CV('TOTAL',SIMC)  = SUM(H, CV(H,SIMC));
5033  *   Alternative definition of CV requiring less precalculations.
5034   CV2(H,SIMC)       = (x_EH(H,SIMC) - EXPSUB(H,SIMC)) - (EH0(H) - EXPSUB(H,'BASE')) * PRICEIND(H,SIMC) / PRICEIND(H,'BASE');
5035   CV2('TOTAL',SIMC) = SUM(H, CV2(H,SIMC));
5036   
       Deriving CV2:
       CV2 =  EH - EXPV0
           =  EH - ((V0/BETA) * PIND + EXPSUB);
           =  EH - EXPSUB - (V0/BETA) * PIND ;
           =  EH - EXPSUB - (1/BETA)*PIND*V0 ;
           = (EH - EXPSUB) - (1/BETA)*PIND *BETA*( EH0 - EXPSUB0 ) / PIND0;
           = (EH - EXPSUB) - PIND *( EH0 - EXPSUB0 ) / PIND0;
           = (EH - EXPSUB) - (EH0 - EXPSUB0) * PIND/PIND0;
       In words: CV = [current supernumerary spending] MINUS [base supernumerary spending adjusted for changes in price index].
5048   
5049  display BETAAUX, betam, betah ;
5050  *------------------------------------------------------------------
5051  *2.5.11  Other reporting: Percentage deviations from base year
5052  *------------------------------------------------------------------
5053   
5054  PARAMETER
5055  *Model parameters (parameters ending with X contain post-solution values)
5056   p_alphavf(F,A,SIM)             productivity shift for factor F in activity A
5057   p_alphava(A,SIM)               shift parameter for CES activity production function
5058   p_betah(A,H,SIM)               marginal share of household consumption on home commodity C from activity A
5059   p_betam(C,H,SIM)               marginal share of household consumption on marketed commodity C
5060   p_gammah(A,H,SIM)              per-capita subsistence consumption for household H on home commodity C for activity A
5061   p_gammam(C,H,SIM)              per-capita subsistence consumption of marketed commodity C for household H
5062   p_icd(C,CP,SIM)                trade input of C per unit of commodity CP produced & sold domestically
5063   p_ice(C,CP,SIM)                trade input of C per unit of commodity CP exported
5064   p_icm(C,CP,SIM)                trade input of C per unit of commodity CP imported
5065   p_pop(H,SIM)                   total population of workers and non-workers in household H
5066   p_pwe(C,SIM)                   world price of exports
5067   p_pwm(C,SIM)                   world price of imports
5068   p_qbarg(C,SIM)                 exogenous (unscaled) government demand
5069   p_qbarinv(C,SIM)               exogenous (unscaled) investment demand
5070   p_qdst(C,SIM)                  inventory investment by sector of origin
5071   p_shif(AC,ACP,SIM)             share of institution INS (incl. rest of world) in post-tax income of factor F
5072   p_shii(AC,ACP,SIM)             share of institution INS in post-tax post-savings income of institution INSP
5073   p_trnsf(F,INS,SIM)             transfers from factor F to institution INS (including rest of world)
5074   p_trnsi(INS,INS,SIM)           transfers from institution INS) to institution INSP (including rest of world)
5075  *Model variables (parameters ending with XP contain percentage deviations from base year values)
5076   p_CPI(SIM)                     consumer price index (PQ-based)
5077   p_DMPS(SIM)                    change in marginal propensity to save for selected institution
5078   p_DPI(SIM)                     index for domestic producer prices (PDS-based)
5079   p_DTA(SIM)                     additive activity tax (TA) adjustment
5080   p_DTE(SIM)                     additive export tax (TE) adjustment
5081   p_DTF(SIM)                     additive direct factor tax (TF) adjustment
5082   p_DTI(SIM)                     additive domestic institution tax (TINS) adjustment
5083   p_DTM(SIM)                     additive import tariff (TM) adjustment
5084   p_DTQ(SIM)                     additive sales tax (TQ) adjustment
5085   p_DTV(SIM)                     additive value-added tax (TVA) adjustment
5086   p_EG(SIM)                      total current government expenditure
5087   p_EH(H,SIM)                    household consumption expenditure
5088   p_EXR(SIM)                     exchange rate
5089   p_FSAV(SIM)                    foreign savings
5090   p_GADJ(SIM)                    government demand scaling factor
5091   p_GOVSHR(SIM)                  government consumption share of absorption
5092   p_GSAV(SIM)                    government savings
5093   p_IADJ(SIM)                    investment scaling factor (for fixed capital formation)
5094   p_INVSHR(SIM)                  investment share of absorption
5095   p_MPS(AC,SIM)                  marginal propensity to save for domestic non-government institution INS
5096   p_MPSADJ(SIM)                  savings rate (MPS) scaling factor
5097   p_PA(A,SIM)                    output price of activity A
5098   p_PDD(C,SIM)                   demand price for commodity C produced and sold domestically
5099   p_PDS(C,SIM)                   supply price for commodity C produced and sold domestically
5100   p_PE(C,SIM)                    price of export aggregate to producer excluding transport costs
5101   p_PM(C,SIM)                    price of import aggregate to demander including transport costs
5102   p_PINTA(A,SIM)                 price of intermediate aggregate
5103   p_PQ(C,SIM)                    price of composite commodity C
5104   p_PSAV(SIM)                    total private savings
5105   p_PVA(A,SIM)                   value added price
5106   p_PX(C,SIM)                    average output price
5107   p_PXAC(A,C,SIM)                price of commodity C from activity A
5108   p_QA(A,SIM)                    level of domestic activity
5109   p_QD(C,SIM)                    quantity of domestic sales
5110   p_QE(C,SIM)                    quantity of exports
5111   p_QF(F,A,SIM)                  quantity demanded of factor F from activity A
5112   p_QFS(F,SIM)                   quantity of factor supply
5113   p_QG(C,SIM)                    quantity of government consumption
5114   p_QH(C,H,SIM)                  quantity consumed of marketed commodity C by household H
5115   p_QHA(A,H,SIM)                 quantity consumed of home activity A by household H
5116   p_QINT(C,A,SIM)                quantity of intermediate demand for commodity C from activity A
5117   p_QINTA(A,SIM)                 quantity of aggregate intermediate input
5118   p_QINV(C,SIM)                  quantity of fixed investment demand
5119   p_QM(C,SIM)                    quantity of imports
5120   p_QQ(C,SIM)                    quantity of composite goods supply
5121   p_QT(C,SIM)                    quantity of trade and transport demand for commodity C
5122   p_QVA(A,SIM)                   quantity of aggregate value added
5123   p_QX(C,SIM)                    quantity of aggregate marketed commodity output
5124   p_QXAC(A,C,SIM)                quantity of ouput of commodity C from activity A
5125   p_TA(A,SIM)                    activity tax rates
5126   p_TAADJ(SIM)                   activity tax (TA) scaling factor
5127   p_TABS(SIM)                    total absorption
5128   p_TE(C,SIM)                    export tax rates
5129   p_TEADJ(SIM)                   export tax (TE) scaling factor
5130   p_TF(F,SIM)                    direct factor tax rates
5131   p_TFADJ(SIM)                   direct factor tax (TF) scaling factor
5132   p_TI(AC,SIM)                   direct tax rates on domestic institutions INSD
5133   p_TIADJ(SIM)                   direct tax (TINS) scaling factor
5134   p_TM(C,SIM)                    import tariff rates
5135   p_TMADJ(SIM)                   import tariff (TM) scaling factor
5136   p_TRII(AC,ACP,SIM)             transfers from column institutions INSF to row institutions INS
5137   p_TQ(C,SIM)                    composite commodity taxes
5138   p_TQADJ(SIM)                   composite commodity tax (TQ) scaling factor
5139   p_TV(A,SIM)                    value-added tax rate
5140   p_TVADJ(SIM)                   value-added tax (TVA) scaling factor
5141   p_WF(F,SIM)                    economywide wage (rent) for factor F
5142   p_WFDIST(F,A,SIM)              wage distortion variable for factor F in activity A
